WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.549 --> 00:00:07.440
Federal courts had previously put two different versions of the ban on hold,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.620
to resolve questions about its constitutionality and on June 26th,

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.880
the Supreme Court ruled that the ban can take effect with some

00:00:15.009 --> 00:00:18.929
limitations before the High Court reviews it in the fall. The ban stops all

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:22.640
tourists from six majority Muslim countries from entering the US

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.700
for at least 90 days and all refugees for at least

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:33.300
120 days, unless they already have a visa or have a bona fide relationship with a person or organization in America.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:37.290
Trump's bans could almost be over by the time the court hears the case.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.780
Trump called the Supreme court's decision a clear victory

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.860
for our national security and he did title it 

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:51.760
The Protection of the Nation from Foreign Terrorist Entry into the United States.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.740
After the first ban was put on hold by a judge in Washington and the Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:57.510
Trump signed

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.060
what was in his words,

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.040
a quote watered-down version with the same title,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.430
but there is absolutely no evidence for such security benefit.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.769
Americans were more likely to be killed by their own clothes than by an immigrant terrorist and

00:01:11.049 --> 00:01:14.580
nobody in the US has been killed in a terrorist attack by immigrants,

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.160
from these particular six nations in the last 40 years.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.760
But while the temporary bans have gotten all the headlines, the order has sent the US

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:27.460
down a path of a fundamental shift in American refugee policy.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.800
reviewing all admitting procedures and

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.160
announcing that in the future,

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:36.800
refugees will only be admitted from countries that ensure the security and welfare of the United States.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.160
This is the opposite of how the US has defined refugees in the past.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:50.540
People fleeing persecution and violence that their own governments cannot or will not protect them from.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:56.199
And this comes at a time when the world faces its biggest refugee crisis since World War II,

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.660
when 1 in 122 people are displaced.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:06.840
So if you're worried about US terrorist attacks like 9/11, San Bernardino, or the Orlando pulse shooting,

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.440
This order would have prevented none of them.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.960
But if you happen to be afraid of Muslims,

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.120
this ban might sound pretty good.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.340
It falls in line with Trump's anti-muslim

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.500
statements, the most famous of which he said during the campaign

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:23.440
Donald J. Trump is calling for a total and complete shutdown

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.620
of Muslims entering the United States.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:32.820
The attitude, that people fleeing terror, war, and famine are dangerous, that attitude

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:38.700
resulted in the infamous decision in June 1939 to turn away the St.Louis, a ship carrying

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.880
937 Jewish Refugees fleeing another hostile government: Hitler's.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:48.320
First they were turned away in Cuba - the liner St. Louis turned back from Cuba,

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.720
then they approached Florida

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.160
but the US coast guard patrolling the waters kept them away too.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.700
They eventually disembarked in Belgium, The Netherlands, and France.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.860
Jewish unfortunates without a country

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:02.320
permitted to land in Belgium after five weeks of suspense afloat.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.360
And hundreds died in Holocaust.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.900
Since then, the US government has been the world's leader in resettling refugees.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.820
They finally reach a haven of safety.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.140
The State Department website still brags about it,

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:18.660
claiming three million refugees resettled around all 50 states since 1975.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.420
The Statue of Liberty bears these goals too.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:22.820
A plaque on the pedestal reads

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:27.780
Give me your tired, your poor, your huddled masses, yearning to breathe free.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.000
The wretched refuse of your teeming shore.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.900
Send these, the homeless

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.600
tempest-tossed to me. I lift my lamp beside the golden door.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:39.720
America hasn't always lived up to these lofty goals

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.260
But since World War II, we've lived in an era

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:47.200
where the diversity of who gets to come to America has gone up and up.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.280
Regardless of where you come from.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.600
The Trump administration has been trying to end this era since January.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.670
After multiple defeats,

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.900
it's won its first battle, and seeing through this vision.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.940
Lawyers and protesters are sure to try to preserve it,

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.620
around the country and this fall in the Supreme Court.

